course: dictionary
lesson: 4012
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. A capital letter is a clue: it often means a name, as in «Garibaldi, and the infant Samuel, and Queen Victoria». Here Garibaldi is a person.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat looks at plaster statues in Mole's home. Ask yourself: is this a thing, a person or clothing? The words around it help. Statues stand for people, so Garibaldi must be a man.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. The statue of Garibaldi stands next to Queen Victoria, so he must be a person, not a biscuit. Always read on to find the clue.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: Giuseppe Garibaldi (1807–1882) was an Italian general who fought to unite Italy, and he was a hero in Victorian Britain.
fact: A garibaldi, with a small g, is a flat rectangular biscuit with squashed currants inside, sometimes called a «squashed fly biscuit».
fact: A garibaldi can also mean a red blouse, like the red shirts worn by Garibaldi's soldiers, the «Redshirts». This is an old fashion.
fact: Victorian and Edwardian homes often had plaster statues of heroes as ornaments.
exercise: «The forecourt has plaster statues of Garibaldi, and the infant Samuel, and Queen Victoria.» In this sentence, what is Garibaldi? | *A person, an Italian hero | A biscuit with squashed currants | A red blouse | A garden seat
exercise: A cook serves a «garibaldi» with tea. What is it? | A plaster statue of a hero | A general who fought in Italy | A roller for the lawn | *A flat biscuit with squashed currants inside
exercise: Why does the word Garibaldi start with a capital G? | It shows the word is a biscuit | *It is the name of a particular person | It is the start of a new sentence every time | It means a red blouse
test: An old book says, «She wore a garibaldi to the party.» What does garibaldi mean? | *A red blouse, like the red shirts of old | A type of fern in a wire basket | A flat biscuit with currants | A plaster statue of a hero
test: Which clue best shows that Garibaldi is a person in «plaster statues of Garibaldi, and the infant Samuel, and Queen Victoria»? | *He is listed with Queen Victoria and other heroes | It is in a garden with ferns | It has a capital letter, so it must be a biscuit | Mole hates his ground being kicked up
exam: Mia reads «The biscuit was named after Garibaldi.» What does Garibaldi mean here? | A plaster statue of Queen Victoria | A red blouse worn by soldiers | *The Italian general Giuseppe Garibaldi | A flat biscuit with currants
